IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc31-forms-periods.l4 -- one helper for part ccc31 (the Forms 24 to 54 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46):
-- how a period that the text measures in years, months and days from a day is counted.
-- The Forms say "for the term of ___", "for a period of ___ days", "for the period of ___ from the date of this order",
-- "every ___ days after you first report". They do not say how a period from a day is counted: the Interpretation Act, which
-- would, is not among the sources. This module fixes ONE counting and says so (NOTES.md, fork F16): the period begins ON the day
-- named and runs for the stated number of years, months and days, so that a period of 30 days from 1 January is 1 to 30 January and
-- ends at the start of 31 January. Years and months are calendar units (`add months` clamps a day that does not exist in the
-- month, so one month from 31 January is 28 February in a common year); "less a day" takes one day off the end ("two years less a day").
-- The name begins `forms 24-54` so that it cannot collide with a name of another part.

§ `Counting a period that begins on a day`

GIVEN start IS A DATE
      period IS A `Length of time`
GIVETH A DATE
`forms 24-54 the first day after a period that begins on a day` MEANS
    (`add months` start ((period's years TIMES 12) PLUS period's months))
        PLUS (period's days MINUS (IF period's `less a day` THEN 1 ELSE 0))

GIVEN day IS A DATE
      start IS A DATE
      period IS A `Length of time`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`forms 24-54 the day falls within a period that begins on a day` MEANS
    day AT LEAST start AND day LESS THAN (`forms 24-54 the first day after a period that begins on a day` start period)
